    dead form.
  - [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps receiving the password and still never puts it in a log call. No change beyond the line above — the
    point is that the allowlist means this is now enforced structurally, not just by care.

  [1mOne thing I'm deliberately not changing:[0m [38;5;153mconsole.log(greet('world'))[39m in [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is the program's [3moutput[0m, not a
  log line. Routing it through the logger would turn [38;5;153mHello, world![39m into a JSON record and break what the program prints.
  It stays a [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m; I'll add a [38;5;153mlogger.debug('greeting emitted', ...)[39m alongside it instead. Conflating output with
  logging is a common way this kind of change breaks a CLI.

  [1mTesting.[0m [38;5;153mcore.js[39m is pure, so its tests need no DOM, no network, and no process — which is what makes the allowlist
  genuinely verifiable. The tests that matter: an unlisted key is dropped; an allowlisted key holding an object is
  dropped; long strings truncate; the denylist scrub catches nested [38;5;153mpassword[39m; level filtering respects each runtime's
  config precedence. I'd also assert that a record containing a password in an unlisted field produces remote output
  with no trace of it — a direct test of the security property rather than of the code shape.

  That brings up tooling, since [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m currently has no dependencies, no scripts, and no test runner. This is the
  cheapest moment to set any of it up, before code exists.

